WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.760
This appendix is deliberately perishable.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:08.240
Everything else in the book is written

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.230
to survive the next model release.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.780
This part is a snapshot,

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.960
and the tools shift every few months.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.730
So instead of naming winners,

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.100
here is a way to judge any

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:17.640
tool,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.790
including the ones that don't exist yet.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.580
When a detail goes stale,

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:22.600
trust the principles,

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.490
and check the vendor's current documentation.

00:00:25.742 --> 00:00:27.302
Coding agents tend to come in three

00:00:27.302 --> 00:00:28.002
shapes.

00:00:28.002 --> 00:00:30.082
Agents in your terminal or editor,

00:00:30.082 --> 00:00:32.652
working in your repository with your permissions.

00:00:32.762 --> 00:00:34.722
That's where most of the book's interactive

00:00:34.722 --> 00:00:35.702
work happens.

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:36.762
Cloud agents,

00:00:36.762 --> 00:00:38.142
where you hand over a task,

00:00:38.142 --> 00:00:39.902
and a pull request comes back from

00:00:39.902 --> 00:00:41.172
a hosted sandbox.

00:00:41.362 --> 00:00:43.262
And kits for building agents of your

00:00:43.262 --> 00:00:43.702
own.

00:00:43.702 --> 00:00:45.832
Most vendors now offer all three.

00:00:46.643 --> 00:00:47.283
Underneath,

00:00:47.283 --> 00:00:48.723
shared conventions are forming.

00:00:48.723 --> 00:00:51.943
An instruction file like agents .md that

00:00:51.943 --> 00:00:53.213
many tools can read.

00:00:53.223 --> 00:00:54.663
A common protocol,

00:00:54.663 --> 00:00:55.683
MCP,

00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:57.863
for connecting agents to tools and data.

00:00:57.863 --> 00:00:58.643
Skills,

00:00:58.643 --> 00:00:59.503
packaged,

00:00:59.503 --> 00:01:01.203
know -how that an agent loads only

00:01:01.203 --> 00:01:02.193
when it needs it.

00:01:02.323 --> 00:01:03.103
And hooks,

00:01:03.103 --> 00:01:05.043
small scripts that run the same way

00:01:05.043 --> 00:01:06.153
every time,

00:01:06.163 --> 00:01:07.913
around the agent's actions.

00:01:08.718 --> 00:01:10.638
Models change fastest of all.

00:01:10.638 --> 00:01:13.258
Frontier commercial models still lead on long,

00:01:13.258 --> 00:01:14.508
multi file work.

00:01:14.678 --> 00:01:17.248
Open weight models have improved dramatically,

00:01:17.258 --> 00:01:19.118
and some run on a well equipped

00:01:19.118 --> 00:01:19.918
laptop.

00:01:19.918 --> 00:01:21.438
Many agent tools can point at a

00:01:21.438 --> 00:01:22.178
local model,

00:01:22.178 --> 00:01:23.798
so a laptop can drive a real

00:01:23.798 --> 00:01:24.648
agent loop.

00:01:24.778 --> 00:01:27.298
Bigger context windows haven't removed the need

00:01:27.298 --> 00:01:28.708
to curate context.

00:01:29.018 --> 00:01:31.378
And agentic work burns through tokens,

00:01:31.378 --> 00:01:32.958
so the cost advice in the book

00:01:32.958 --> 00:01:33.888
still applies.

00:01:34.818 --> 00:01:36.578
So here are the questions to ask

00:01:36.578 --> 00:01:37.448
any tool.

00:01:37.478 --> 00:01:38.698
What can it see?

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:39.938
What can it touch?

00:01:39.938 --> 00:01:41.328
How does it get feedback?

00:01:41.438 --> 00:01:43.078
How do you review its work?

00:01:43.078 --> 00:01:44.718
And what happens when it reads something

00:01:44.718 --> 00:01:45.568
hostile?

00:01:46.126 --> 00:01:48.336
That last question isn't theoretical.

00:01:48.366 --> 00:01:50.706
Public incidents have shown agents with broad

00:01:50.706 --> 00:01:53.366
access leaking private data after reading a

00:01:53.366 --> 00:01:55.466
malicious issue or support ticket.

00:01:55.466 --> 00:01:57.386
The write ups are short and worth

00:01:57.386 --> 00:01:57.946
reading.

00:01:57.946 --> 00:01:59.446
And measure your results,

00:01:59.446 --> 00:02:02.166
because feeling fast and being fast are

00:02:02.166 --> 00:02:03.316
not the same thing.

00:02:04.095 --> 00:02:06.435
Answer those five questions and the product

00:02:06.435 --> 00:02:07.675
names stop mattering.

00:02:07.675 --> 00:02:09.695
The names on this page will change.

00:02:09.695 --> 00:02:11.895
The principles in the chapters will outlast

00:02:11.895 --> 00:02:12.995
every one of them.

00:02:12.995 --> 00:02:14.795
That's the whole crew and the whole

00:02:14.795 --> 00:02:15.415
ship.

00:02:15.415 --> 00:02:16.475
Read the whole book,

00:02:16.475 --> 00:02:17.215
free,

00:02:17.215 --> 00:02:19.545
at theagenticcrew .com.
